An address obtained by taking \texttt{\&ticks} is the address of a particular instance. It is not a recipe that another thread can use to find its own instance, and the address must not be used after the owning thread's storage has ended. In the example, software that needs to find \texttt{ticks} for the current thread should evaluate the TLS access anew, or invoke a routine that does so.

For object-file evidence, save the following as \texttt{tls.c}:

\begin{verbatim}
_Thread_local int ticks = 7;
_Thread_local int scratch;
int step(void) { return ++ticks + scratch; }
\end{verbatim}

\begin{verbatim}
for m in global-dynamic initial-exec local-exec; do
  gcc -O2 -fPIC -mtls-dialect=gnu -ftls-model="$m" \
      -c tls.c -o "$m.o"
  readelf -Wr "$m.o"
done
gcc -shared -o libcounter.so global-dynamic.o
readelf -Wl libcounter.so
gcc -shared -o bad.so local-exec.o   # expected link failure
\end{verbatim}

With local GCC and GNU binutils this yields \texttt{TLSGD} (and a call relocation for the helper), \texttt{GOTTPOFF}, and \texttt{TPOFF32}, respectively. The resulting \texttt{libcounter.so} has a TLS header with file size \(4\) and memory size \(8\). The last link fails on \texttt{TPOFF32}: asking for \texttt{-fPIC} does not repair the explicitly selected local-exec model. This failure checks the boundary between what a shared object can describe and what only its eventual executable or loader can decide.
